course: biology
lesson: 376
title: The Chornobyl zone: how wild animals came back, and what is still argued
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In 1986 a reactor at the Chornobyl power plant in Ukraine exploded and spread radiation, and people had to leave a large area around it. Picture a town where nobody has walked for 40 years: birches grow through flats, moss covers the roads.
explain: With no hunting, farming or traffic, wolves, elk, boar, lynx and even brown bears came back. Camera traps film them walking down empty streets, and wild Przewalski's horses shelter in old barns.
explain: Radiation still harms living things in the worst spots, and scientists argue how much. Both things can be true: radiation hurts animals, yet people being gone helps them more in many places.
explain: Real Chornobyl animals look normal: a wolf there has one head and four legs. Games and films invent monsters because they are fun and scary, not because they are true.
fact: In 1986 a reactor at the Chornobyl power plant exploded, and people were moved out of a large area around it.
fact: The zone in Ukraine is about 2,600 square kilometres, and there is a zone on the Belarus side too; without people, forests and meadows grew back into villages and fields.
fact: Wolves, elk, deer, wild boar, lynx, foxes and even brown bears now live in the zone, and a study on the Belarus side counted about seven times more wolves than in nearby reserves.
fact: In 1998 about 30 Przewalski's horses were let go in the zone; they grew to over a hundred and use old barns as shelter from heat and flies, filmed by cameras hidden in the buildings.
fact: Some scientists find fewer insects and spiders and more sick birds in the most radioactive spots, while others find animal numbers in the whole zone similar to or higher than in clean reserves.
fact: The pine trees nearest the reactor were killed by radiation and turned red-brown, giving the Red Forest its name; new birches and pines have grown back since.
fact: In the most radioactive spots fallen leaves rot slowly, which scientists think is because the tiny decomposers that break down dead things, such as fungi, germs and minibeasts, are harmed.
fact: Spanish scientists reported that eastern tree frogs in the zone are darker than usual; they think the dark pigment melanin may have helped frogs survive, and it is still being studied.
fact: Hundreds of dogs in the zone come from pets left behind in 1986; scientists study their DNA, and volunteers vaccinate and feed them.
fact: Russian troops crossed the zone in 2022 and dug trenches in the most radioactive soil of the Red Forest; fires, mines and damage hurt the reserve, but Ukrainian rangers and scientists returned to keep watching.
fact: Mushrooms suck up caesium, a radioactive metal the explosion left in the soil, very well, and wild boar eat underground fungi, so boar in Germany can still be radioactive long after other meat is clean.
fact: Nobody should pick mushrooms or berries in the zone.
fact: There are no giant mutants: animals badly harmed in 1986 mostly died young, and the animals seen today are healthy ones.
exercise: What happened at Chornobyl in 1986? | A reactor at the power plant exploded and people were moved out | A big earthquake hit Kyiv and broke many of the old buildings in the city | A forest fire burned all of Ukraine | A meteorite fell on a village
exercise: How big is the Chornobyl zone on the Ukrainian side? | About 2,600 square kilometres | About 2 square kilometres in all | About the size of all of Ukraine | About the size of a football field
exercise: Which big animals now live in the zone? | Wolves, elk, wild boar, lynx and brown bears | Lions, zebras and giraffes from a zoo that escaped | Polar bears and penguins | Only rats and pigeons
exercise: Why did many big animals do well in the zone? | There is no hunting, farming or traffic | Radiation makes animals stronger and healthier | Visitors feed them every single day of the year | It is much warmer there than anywhere else
exercise: What did a study on the Belarus side find about wolves? | About seven times more wolves than in nearby reserves | No wolves at all, because the radiation killed every single one | Wolves with two heads | Only baby wolves
exercise: How many Przewalski's horses were let go in the zone in 1998? | About 30 | Only about 3 | About 3,000 | About 300,000
exercise: Why do wild horses in the zone use old barns? | For shelter from heat and flies | To sleep in hay beds made by people | To hide from the radiation outside | To be milked by the park rangers
exercise: Some studies find fewer insects in the worst spots, others find as many animals as in clean reserves. What can be true? | Both: radiation harms, but people being gone helps more in many places | Only the first, so the second team must be lying | Only the second, because radiation does nothing at all to any living thing | Neither, nobody has studied it
exercise: Why is it called the Red Forest? | Pines near the reactor were killed by radiation and turned red-brown | The trees have red flowers | Red foxes live there | It was painted red by soldiers as a warning so that nobody would go in
exercise: Why do fallen leaves rot slowly in the most radioactive spots? | The tiny decomposers that break them down are harmed | Leaves there are made of a tough kind of plastic that never rots | It is always frozen there | Animals eat the leaves first
exercise: What did Spanish scientists report about tree frogs in the zone? | They are darker than usual | They are bright pink | They glow in the dark | They have three eyes
exercise: Where do the dogs of the Chornobyl zone come from? | Pets left behind in 1986 | Wolves that turned into dogs | Soldiers brought them in 2022 | They were bred in a secret lab
exercise: Why is digging trenches in Red Forest soil dangerous? | The most radioactive dust is stirred up and breathed or carried | Trenches attract wolves | The soil is too hard to dig with spades and machines | The old dead trees fall on the diggers and the trenches cave in on them
exercise: Why can wild boar in Germany still be radioactive? | They eat underground fungi that suck up radioactive caesium | They swim all the way up the Chornobyl river from Germany every summer | They were born in Ukraine | They eat radioactive grass only
exercise: Why should nobody pick mushrooms in the zone? | Mushrooms suck up radioactive caesium from the soil | Mushrooms there are all poisonous kinds | They are owned by the rangers | They are too big to carry
exercise: Are there giant mutant monsters in the Chornobyl zone? | No, the animals seen today look normal | Yes, there are three-headed wolves and giant elk | Yes, giant spiders as big as dogs live there | Only at night, when they come out to hunt
test: Count them: a video game shows a three-headed wolf from the zone. How many heads has a real Chornobyl wolf? | 1 | 3 | 2 | 0
test: Count them: a camera trap shows 2 elk and 3 boar. How many legs? | 20 | 10 | 15 | 25
test: Count them: about 30 horses were let go in 1998 and now there are over 100. By how many times did they grow, at least? | More than 3 times | 30 times | 2 times | 100 times, one for every horse
test: «chornobyl = dead zone, nothing lives there» What is right? | Wolves, elk, boar, lynx, bears and horses live there | It is a desert with no life of any kind | Only mutant monsters with extra legs and eyes live there | Only plants live there
test: «radiation must be GOOD for animals then, look how many» What is the careful answer? | Radiation harms living things; animals do well mostly because people are gone | Yes, radiation makes animals healthy and lets them have more babies than usual | Yes, radiation makes animals bigger | Radiation has no effect at all
test: How could two careful studies of the zone seem to disagree? | One looks at the worst spots, the other at the whole zone | One of the scientists must be lying about what he saw there | Animals hide from one team only | Radiation changes the numbers on cameras
test: What would happen to a road if nobody used it for 40 years? | Plants would crack it and a forest would grow over it | It would stay brand new | It would turn into a river | It would sink into the ground at once and leave a long deep hole
test: «there's dogs at chornobyl, they look friendly, i'll cuddle one» What is wiser? | They are still strange animals, so do not touch them | They are tame pets, so cuddle away | They glow, so wear thick gloves and then hug them | They are wolves in disguise
test: Why would a deep-digging animal like a boar carry more radiation than a grazing deer? | It eats underground fungi that pile up caesium | Boar are born radioactive | Deer wear thicker fur that keeps the radiation out | Boar drink more rain
test: What would happen to a forest floor if nothing rotted the leaves? | Dead leaves would pile up and feed fewer new plants | The leaves would turn back into trees | The forest would grow faster than ever before | The leaves would fly away in the wind
test: Why might darker skin have helped tree frogs after the accident? | Melanin may protect from some harm, so dark frogs had more babies | Dark frogs are harder for radiation to find because it cannot see them | Dark frogs ate the radiation | Dark skin keeps frogs cold
test: In a film a girl drinks from a river in the zone and becomes a superhero. What is right? | Radiation harms the body and gives no powers | Radiation gives powers to children only | River water there is clean and special | Superpowers come from caesium
exam: Identify the principal reason for which populations of large mammals have increased within the Chornobyl exclusion zone. | The absence of hunting, agriculture and traffic following the evacuation | A beneficial influence of radiation upon their health and their fertility | The deliberate feeding of animals by the authorities | A marked warming of the regional climate
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Red Forest. | Its pines were killed by radiation and turned red-brown; younger trees have since grown | Its trees bear red flowers throughout the year | It was so named on account of the great numbers of red foxes which reside within it | It was painted red to serve as a warning
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the radioactivity of wild boar in Germany? | It persists because boar consume subterranean fungi which accumulate radioactive caesium | It arises because the boar migrated from Ukraine across Poland in great numbers after 1986 | It derives from grass alone | It has entirely disappeared since 1990
exam: Ascertain which of the following assertions concerning animals of the zone is in accordance with scientific evidence. | The animals observed today are of normal appearance, and no giant mutants exist | Wolves bearing several heads are frequently observed by the rangers of the zone | Animals of the zone emit visible light | All animals of the zone are sterile
